WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.920
Binary input is easy to underestimate because it does not announce itself in words.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:15.060
A file, packet, firmware image, database page, or custom record may appear to be just a sequence of bytes, but those bytes usually represent structured information.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:25.760
They may encode headers, field lengths, offsets, counts, flags, checksums, records, embedded strings, and commands that later code will rely on.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.140
In C, the program does not get automatic protection from a binary format.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:39.640
The compiler will not know whether a length is reasonable, whether an offset points inside the input, whether a field was truncated, or whether a count would overflow an allocation.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:46.900
The parser has to make those checks explicitly before it treats bytes as fields, records, objects, or decisions.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.360
The consequences of weak binary validation are not limited to security defects.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:01:02.700
Malformed or incomplete data can crash a process, corrupt internal state, cause excessive allocation, produce misleading output, or make later diagnostics almost impossible.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:15.560
A parser that works only for perfect input is not robust enough for real systems, because real systems see partial transfers, old versions, damaged files, unexpected producers, and operational mistakes.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.220
Many binary parser failures begin with a small act of trust.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.960
Code trusts a declared size before checking that the bytes exist.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.820
It trusts an offset before checking the full range.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.080
It trusts a layout before considering alignment and padding.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.860
It trusts a count before checking multiplication.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.240
Defensive parsing reverses that order.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:39.980
The program first confirms that the input satisfies the format rule, and only then uses the value.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.040
A useful mindset is to treat every byte as untrusted until the specific rule that gives it meaning has been checked.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.060
That does not mean the code has to be complicated.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.960
It means validation is deliberate, local, and reviewable.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:02:03.180
Good binary parsing makes the contract visible: what must be present, what values are acceptable, what relationships must hold, and how the parser fails when the contract is not met.
